# diff with telcorag paper: 
Two datasets were used: a synthetic optimization set of 2,000 MCQs generated from 3GPP Rel-18 documents, and the TeleQnA evaluation set of 1,840 MCQs. The options (A/B/C/D) were deliberately excluded from the retrieval process and only used to check whether the final answer was correct — the authors note explicitly that MCQ format doesn't reflect real-world query patterns, so they didn't want the options to influence retrieval.
A few things worth noting for your chapter's TelcoRAG comparison:
What accuracy captures: whether the system picks the right MCQ option — a fairly narrow signal. It says nothing about faithfulness (is the answer grounded in retrieved chunks?), answer completeness, or whether the retrieved chunks were actually the right ones.
What it misses entirely: retrieval quality is only measured indirectly, via the downstream MCQ score. There's no separate measurement of whether the correct 3GPP chunks were retrieved — the NN router accuracy (Table IV) measures series-level routing, not chunk-level relevance.
Contrast with your approach: your layered evaluation framework — keyword scoring, RAGAS faithfulness, RAGAS answer relevancy, and planned ground truth benchmarking — directly separates retrieval quality from generation quality, which TelcoRAG doesn't do. That's a meaningful methodological distinction worth calling out in your Background chapter's "TelcoRAG Research Context" section.

